<h2>What the statutes actually test</h2>
<p>Each maintenance provision frames its threshold around the claimant, not her family tree. Section 144(1) BNSS speaks of a wife who is &ldquo;unable to maintain herself&rdquo;; Section 24 HMA of a spouse who has &ldquo;no independent income sufficient for her or his support&rdquo;; Section 18 HAMA entitles a Hindu wife to be maintained by her husband during her lifetime; Section 20 of the DV Act directs monetary relief to meet the expenses incurred and losses suffered by the aggrieved person. The operative words are <em>herself</em> and <em>independent income</em>. A father&rsquo;s fixed deposits are not the wife&rsquo;s income by any definition; she cannot spend them, sue for them, or compel their continuance. During her parents&rsquo; lifetime she has no enforceable right to their property at all — an expectancy of inheritance is not means.</p>

<h2>Why voluntary support cannot discharge a statutory duty</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h3>The duty is personal</h3><p>The obligation to maintain a wife rests on the husband by force of statute. It is not a residual duty that activates only when every other human being who might feed her has refused.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Charity is precarious</h3><p>Parental support can stop tomorrow — on the father&rsquo;s death, the brother&rsquo;s marriage, a change in family fortunes. A maintenance order cannot be built on, or denied because of, a source the claimant does not control.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>The incentive problem</h3><p>If natal support defeated maintenance, every husband would be rewarded for driving his wife back to her parents — the law would subsidise the very abandonment it exists to remedy.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>No reciprocal obligation</h3><p>Parents owe no legal duty to maintain a married daughter; the HAMA obligation covers minor children and the unmarried daughter. The husband&rsquo;s argument asks the court to enforce against her parents a duty the law never imposed on them.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Where the natal family legitimately enters the case</h2>
<p>Precision matters, because the argument is not wrong everywhere — it is wrong as a disqualifier. There are three narrow, legitimate uses. First, <strong>quantum of the housing component</strong>: where the wife is presently residing with her parents, the court assessing her monthly needs may weigh how the residence element is currently met — though the husband remains answerable for her residence, and nothing prevents her from seeking to live independently. Second, <strong>the wife&rsquo;s own assets</strong>: property or income standing in her name — including what her family has already transferred to her — is genuinely hers and properly counts in the means assessment. The line is ownership: what is hers counts, what is theirs does not. Third, <strong>credibility</strong>: a claimant who pleads destitution while demonstrably funding substantial litigation or lifestyle must explain the source; if the explanation is family support, it should have been disclosed in the affidavit of assets from the start.</p>

<h2>The disclosure discipline</h2>
<p>Maintenance courts across the country work on comprehensive affidavits of assets and liabilities from both sides, and the formats ask about support from family members in terms. The temptation on the wife&rsquo;s side is to omit such support for fear it will be used against her; the temptation on the husband&rsquo;s side is to inflate it into a disqualification. Both are errors. Declared support explains survival during years of litigation and underlines the husband&rsquo;s default — someone else has been performing his obligation. Concealed support, once exposed in cross-examination on bank statements, infects the claimant&rsquo;s credibility on income, needs and everything else. The sound course is complete candour paired with the correct legal submission: disclosed family assistance is a stop-gap performed by volunteers, not a satisfaction of the respondent&rsquo;s duty.</p>

<h2>Framing the argument on each side</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><h3>The failing version</h3><p>&ldquo;Her father owns two properties and she lives in his house; the petition should be dismissed.&rdquo; This confuses the family&rsquo;s means with the claimant&rsquo;s, invites the court to enforce a non-existent parental duty, and concedes by implication that the husband is paying nothing.</p></div>
  <div class="arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="col new"><h3>The legitimate version</h3><p>&ldquo;The claimant holds disclosed assets and income of her own of X; her stated monthly needs double-count residence she admits is currently met; and her litigation expenditure exceeds her declared means, which goes to credibility.&rdquo; Every element addresses her means and needs — the only questions the statute asks.</p></div>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> For claimants&rsquo; counsel, plead the natal support honestly and characterise it immediately — a loan or stop-gap assistance rendered because of the respondent&rsquo;s default, with particulars where the family expects repayment. For respondents&rsquo; counsel, abandon the rich-father submission as a threshold defence; redirect the same facts to quantum and credibility, where they are admissible and occasionally decisive. Courts have little patience for the proposition that a man&rsquo;s statutory duty shrinks because his wife&rsquo;s parents did not let her starve.</p>
</div>
